</figure>
<figure id="S8.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12527v1/adversarial_skeletons.svg" id="S8.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:439/327;" width="439" height="327"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>Risk-coverage curves under different skeleton types. VSD and human-designed skeletons perform best. Performance degrades gracefully under increasingly strong adversarial attacks, highlighting the importance of a meaningful skeleton.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S8.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12527v1/runtime_scaling.svg" id="S8.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:384/286;" width="384" height="286"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>Empirical runtime overhead across different model sizes. While absolute overhead increases with model size, the relative overhead remains below 20%.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S8.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12527v1/sample_size_sensitivity.svg" id="S8.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:384/288;" width="384" height="288"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span>Coverage and selective risk as a function of sample size (<math id="S8.F10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>). As more samples are used for the information budget, coverage improves and risk decreases, demonstrating estimator stability.</figcaption>
</figure>
